A||N||10||Homer||EGR Tube thingy????||||9||
Z||000000||Homer||03-25-2001||12:32 AM||||  Alrighty then, this is my first post to this here site.  And it's a doosie.<P>I was recently warming up my '86 2m6 SE and my wife came running into the house yelling "YOUR ***** 'N FIERO's ON FIRE!!!!" to which I replied "Your outta your mind woman!" So I ran out side and well it was throwing up some grey'ish colord clouds in the air...and it was making some really funky clunk'n sounds...so I shut her down and opend the lid.  Man there was fluid everywhere, my coolant was all over the ground and the smell...eeewwww. So I let it cool down for like 2 days and went back to servey the damage... Hmmm not to bad some smoked connectors (replaced em) one really messed up rear inlet intermediate hose (which no one seems to have)and some melted spark-plug wire tips...so I set out to fix it. Now as I start ripping things out I see some more damage. Seems that it all started not from the hose that had a big ole bubble in it but from a bad coolant temp sensor. And thinking back to when I first bought the car and had it checked out like a year ago the mechaninc said "wow your car souldn't be idleing at 2200rpm" so he replaced the iac & the o2 sensor and brought the idle down to about 1600rpm he said it was still to high and he thinks that there is a vacuum leak under the upper plenum...So I figure while I'm tearing everything apart why not look...Well now I have hoses that I don't know where they go and some connectors that look like they should have had something connected to them but don't and I found the leak that the guy was talking about. There is a tube that goes from the underside of the plenum to the egr valve. It's a flexy tube surrounded by heat resistant something or other, it looks like one of my mom's old oven mits sewn onto this pipe, well to make a long story longer, it was busted right up next to the plenum....can you say "HUGE VACUUM LEAK" here is a picture of it if you've read this far.(http://www.secureleadercom.com/store/images/67017.GIF) Nobody has the damn thing.  Whew, now can somebody help me put my car back together. I have both the chilton & haynes manuals and niether show the vacuum tube info.<BR>Thank you for reading this and any help you can give.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www2.unikix.com/azfieros/glosslogo.GIF" TARGET=_blank>http://www2.unikix.com/azfieros/glosslogo.GIF</A> ||||reg||9
Z||000001||daved||03-25-2001||09:52 AM||||Homer, here is a few sources for that pipe:<BR>Part #10137917 pipe kit. <BR>FOCOA has one that supposedly will never break for around $79. <BR>Also the Fiero Store has them. <BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homer||03-25-2001||05:16 PM||||Hey thannks for the info, I found earlier that the fiero store has them for $94.00 and a place called "fiero parts" has them for around $72 so I was ordering it from them and at the very end of the transaction a little window popped up that said "thanks for your order" "please expect 2-4 weeks for delivery" holly crap I need this thing like yesterday.  Who is FOCOA?||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||03-25-2001||05:51 PM||||If you can find someone that is good with an oxyacetyene torch you can braze the tube where it is cracked. It has always work for me when I repair them and its a lot cheaper.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||03-26-2001||12:15 AM||||I don't know how much it costs, and I don't have their number, but IRM has a braided stainless steel EGR tube replacement.<BR>Do a search to find IRM's number and a website with scanned pages from their catalog.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||03-26-2001||12:19 AM||||Well, you could do what I did for a temporary fix.  I took the broken flange off the plenum, and siliconed a piece of metal to fill the hole and put the flange back on.  Effectively blocks off the EGR, and with the broken tube in place, you have the illusion of it being hooked up (emissions visual inspection).  It at least gets rid of the enormous vacuum leak until you can do a proper repair.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Homer||03-26-2001||12:41 AM||||Hey all of that is great info..<P>Here is what I did....I cleaned off the 1 inch of carbon and made it all nice and shinney again. Then I took some industrial strength epoxy puddy and put humpty dumpty back together again. Works great just have to hope my engine doesn't reach 400 degrees.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rocketmad.gif"> <BR>  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rocketmad.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Homer (edited 03-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||03-26-2001||11:43 AM||||I believe Paul McKibben (or maybe it was Paul Vargas) has an article somewhere that explains how to modify an egr tube from a pontiac 6000 2.8l engine or a camaro 2.8l.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mshill||03-26-2001||01:12 PM||||Homer,  I had the same issue with my EGR tube and tried JB Weld on it.  It held for a little while but soon developed small cracks and the ECM started throwing the famous code 32.  I had to replace mine altogether.  I have used the EGR tube from a Camaro with good results.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jules||03-27-2001||06:37 PM||||"YOUR ***** 'N FIERO's ON FIRE!!!!" <BR>I've known this to happen, especially on headlight motors.<P>This tube you're having trouble with...I sent mine to a shop to be replaced & it cost me almost $300!!! and I had no fire, all it needed was put on! That hose alone sent my gas mileage from 12 mpg to 26. good luck<P>||||reg||1
